########################################################################
#
### Instructions for setting up and running MLCP                     ###
#
########################################################################

1) Copy an appropriate configuration file from the 'config/' directory
   and edit as appropriate; rename the file as 'arch.mk' in the main
   MLCP directory. Then compile MLCP using

   $ make

   Alternatively, MLCP comes with BLAS and LAPACK libraries included
   (in the 'lib/' directory) which can be compiled and linked
   using e.g. gfortran tools. After performing step 1 above, one can
   use the script

   $ bash install

   to compile and link the libraries and to build MLCP.

2) MLCP requires an input file containing the calculation settings and
   the layer structure of the system of interest. A number of potential
   files containg the force constants are also read from the <pes_path>
   directory. These files are named 'f<order><system>.dat', where
   <order> is the order of the force constants (e.g quadratic, cubic...)
   and <system> corresponds to the 'system' label in the input file.

   To run MLCP, at the command line execute:

   $ mlcp.x <input file>

   The input file consists of five sections, each of which is initiated
   by a '$<section>' tag and terminated with an '$end-<section>' tag.

   $control

       This section sets control parameters for the calculation and can
       be used to set global defaults for settings used for the nodes:

       - 'system' (string): name of the potential energy surface to use.
          Systems 'dummy', 'Henon' (modified Henon-Heiles), and 'CpOsc'
          (2D coupled oscillators are built-in for testing and require 
          no potential files.

       - 'pes_path' (string, default='pes'): path to search for potental
          energy constant files

       - 'resfile' (string): name for files to write restart data to/read 
          restart data from. Setting to 'none' bypasses writing
          restart files; otherwise the following files are written:
             <resfile>_1_eigv.rst, XXXXX_2_eigv.rst (two copies of eigenvalues)
             <resfile>_1_oper.rst, XXXXX_2_oper.rst (two copies of operators)
             <resfile>_1_psi.rst, XXXXX_2_psi.rst   (two copies of wavefunction blocks)
             <resfile>_layers.rst                   (copy of input file used in run)
             <resfile>_act_node_<nid>               (file storing activation counter
                                                     for node <nid> if activations are
                                                     used for this node)
             (two copies of eigv, oper and psi are generated in case of system
             failure during a write, which would result in a corrupt file)

       - 'pe_transform (string): Type of potential energy surface to transform
          to use. Current options include:
          'none' - no transform, i.e. use polynomial potential (default)
          'morse-tanh' - transform asymmetric potentials into Morse variables and
              symmetric potentials into tanh variables
          'poly-tanh' - use polynomial variables for asymmetric potential and
              symmetric potentials into tanh variables

       - 'pe_trans_fac' (float, default='1.d0'): scaling factor for alpha when 
          pe_transform is set to either 'morse-tanh' or 'poly-tanh'. Larger
          values decrease the amount of coupling between modes far from the
          equilibrium geometry in the transformed potential.

       - 'rs' (integer array): an array of 33 integers used to initialize the
          random number generator (default= all zeros)

   $resort 

       This section sets the order of the modes in the tree, where the
       numbering corresponds to that in the potential files. Modes must
       be entered as an array of integers appearing on a single line

   $basis 

       This section sets the numbers of basis functions to be used at each node.
       The first line lists the numbers of 1D harmonic oscillator functions to use
          for single-coordinate nodes at the bottom of the tree.
       Subsequent lines list numbers of n-D eigenfunctions to retain for nodes 
          in the 2nd, 3rd, ... layers. For each node, the values entered must not
          exceed the direct-product basis sizes of the sub-nodes of that node.
       Constraints on the basis set in the '$node' section may cause these values
          being overwritten with values that satisfy the constraints
       The $basis section must be entered as a set of integer arrays, with each
          line corresponding to a layer, in ascending order, beginnng with the
          bottom layer.

   $layers 

       This section defines the grouping of the coordinates by nodes and sub nodes.
       Each entry denotes the number of nodes in the previous layer to be contained
          as sub-nodes in the current layer.
       The $layers section must be entered as a set of integer arrays, with each
          line corresponding to a layer, in ascending order, beginnng with the
          second-to-bottom layer.

   $node

       This section sets control settings for individual nodes. Settings for a
       node must be specified on a single line containing the 'layer-mode'
       designation. Unless otherwise indicated, the following settings can also
       be given in $control above. If a setting appears in both $control and in
       $node, then the latter takes precedence.

       - 'layer-mode' (string, required in $node, not allowed in $control):
          entered as '<layer>-<mode>', with <layer> and <mode> given as integers;
          this specifies the layer and mode for which to apply the settings 
          on the current line.
          Setting <mode> to a value of 0 applies the settings on the line to ALL
             modes contained in <layer>. In this case, these settings can be 
             superseded for individual nodes in <layer> by additional lines in
             $node for said individual values of <mode>.

      - 'donode' (logical, default='T'): perform the calculation for the node.
         Note that setting this to 'F' for a node suppresses calculation for 
         all nodes that are dependent on the results of this node.

      - 'max_nmode' (integer, default='-1', not allowed in $control):
         Maximum number of coupled modes in product functions generated by Guess module

      - 'max_sum' (integer, default='-1', not allowed in $control):
         Maximum sum of quantum numbers in product functions generated by Guess module

      - 'max_qn' (integer, default='-1', not allowed in $control):
         Maximum value of quantum numbers in product functions generated by Guess module

      - 'Etarget' (float, default='0.d0', not allowed in $control):
         Target energy (above the Zero Point Energy) for functions generated in the Guess
            module and (if applicable) for the Solver module

      - 'activation' (string, default='scale-linear', not allowed in $control):
         Type of activation schedule to use when "activating" the coupled Hamiltonian
            from the uncoupled Hamiltonian. Valid choices include:
            'scale-linear': multiply coupling terms in Hamiltonian by iact/nactivations
            'scale-log2': multiply coupling terms in Hamiltonian by 2**(iact-nactivations)
            'scale-loge': multiply coupling terms in Hamiltonian by exp(iact-nactivations)
            'scale-logphi': multiply coupling terms in Hamiltonian by phi**(iact-nactivations),
                where phi is the Golden Ratio = 2.d0/(1.d0+sqrt(5.d0))
            'scale-log10': multiply coupling terms in Hamiltonian by 10**(iact-nactivations)
            'cut-log2': truncate coupling terms smaller than 'hcut' * 2**(iact-nactivations)
            'cut-loge': truncate coupling terms smaller than 'hcut' * exp(iact-nactivations)
            'cut-logphi': truncate coupling terms smaller than 'hcut' * phi**(iact-nactivations),
                where phi is the Golden Ratio = 2.d0/(1.d0+sqrt(5.d0))
            'cut-log10': truncate coupling terms smaller than 'hcut' * 10**(iact-nactivations)

      - 'nactivations' (integer, default='1', not allowed in $control):
         Number of activation steps to use.

      - 'hcut' (float, default='0.d0'): truncate Hamiltonian coupling terms smaller than 'hcut'

      - 'verbosity' (integer, default='0'): controls verbosity of printed output.
         Valid values are: (less verbose) <- [0,1,2] -> (more verbose)

      - 'algo' (integer, default='-1'): parallel algorithm to use. Valid options:
           '-1' legacy parallelism (MPI+OpenMP over vectors). At present, this is the only
                option for nodes with 2 sub-modes when SVD is used for rank-reduction
            '0' CPU (MPI over vectors; OpenMP kernels on CPU)
            '1' GPU (MPI over vectors; OpenACC kernels on GPU)

      - 'lowmem' (integer, default='2'): memory-saving option.
           When algo='-1', options range from 0 (fastest, most memory usage)
                                           to 3 (slowest, least memory usage)
           When algo='0,1' options include 1 (replicate Hamiltonian on each MPI rank)
                                       and 3 (partition Hamiltonian over MPI ranks)
      - 'h_sort_alg' (string, default='pack'): algorithm to compress Hamiltonian by
           collecting common terms.
           'sort': sort terms by mode and collect largest groups; decrease size of group
                   to collect by a factor of 2 each sweep (fastest sorting algorithm)
           'pack': sort terms by mode and collect largest groups; decrease size of group
                   to collect by 1 each sweep (usually gives best compression)
           'compare': compare terms one-by-one and sum if all factors except one are
                   equal (legacy algorithm, should be used only for testing)

      - 'calcbounds' (string, default='calc'): method for computing spectral bounds
           'calc': calculate spectral bounds; if restarting, then read from file
           'recalc': calculate spectral bounds; if restarting, then recompute bounds
           'guess': guess spectral bounds using energies from uncoupled Hamiltonian
                    (saves computational resources but bounds may be inaccurate)

      - 'padbounds' (float, default = '0.d0'): pad the computed spectral bounds at each
           end by this value; a suitable  may improve solver convergence if the bounds
           are guessed or computed to lower accuracy than the target states.

      - 'solver' (string, default='powr'): solver type to use. Acceptable values are:
           'powr': power method
           'pals': intertwined power method ("intertwining")
                   (for modes with D=2, setting red2D='SVD' implies
                    'powr' for those modes)
           'iitn': inverse iteration, users normal equation linear solver
           'iitf': inverse iteration, uses fast linear solver
           'fold': folded spectrum iteration
           The following solvers are experimental and are currently only implemented
              for algo='-1':
           'RQIn': Rayleigh Quotient iteration, uses normal equation linear solver
           'RQIf': Rayleigh Quotient iteration, uses fast linear solver
           'RQIt': intertwined Rayleigh Quotient iteration

      - 'solvtol' (float, default='1.d-10'): solver tolerence; calculation exits early if
           abs((E_prev-iteration - E_this-iteration)/E_this-iteration) < solvtol for ALL eigenvalues

      - 'ncycle' (integer, default='10'): number of cycles. One cycle implies 'npow' power iterations
           + (optional) orthogonalization + (optional) subspace diagonalization. After each
           cycle, energies are printed, convergence is tested, and wavefunction restarts are
           written, if requested.
       
      - 'npow' (integer, default='10'): number of power/intertwining/inverse iterations per cycle

      - 'orthogalg' (string, default='gram'): orthogonalization algorithm, performed each cycle
         after iterations and before subspace diagonalization.
           'gram': Gram-Schmidt orthogonalization
           'S-1/2': Orthogonalize by multiplying by the inverse square root of the overlap matrix
           'none': skip orthogonalization

      - 'diag' (logical, default='T'): perform subspace diagonalization at end of cycle
         (recommended)

      - 'reduceHQ' (logical, default='T'): reduce rank of result of matrix-vector product H*Q
         from (rank-of-H)*psirank to psirank before computing overlaps with other vectors in 
         the subspace. This can substantially reduce the cost of computing the Q^T*H*Q matrix
         but may introduce errors in the matrix elements, particularly for small psirank values

      - 'update' (logical, default='T'): Replace vectors with eigenvectors of subspace Hamiltonian
         at the end of each cycle (recommended). Setting to 'F' causes vectors to be sorted by 
         Rayleigh Quotient.

      - 'ovrlpenalty' (float, default='0.d0'): Tikhonov regularization penalty for solving
         generalized eigenvalue problem during subspace diagonalization. 

      - 'red2D' (string, default='SVD'): rank-reduction algorithm for nodes containing TWO
         submodes.
           'SVD': singular value decomposition (recommended)
           'ALS': alternating least squares
           'SR1': successive rank-1 approximations
           'OPS': orthogonal rank-1 projections plus sorting (experimental)

      - 'redND' (string, default='ALS'): type of rank-reduction to use for modes containing MORE
         THAN TWO submodes. Includes same choices as 'red2D' except that SVD is not included

      - 'psirank' (integer, default='10'): target reducion rank for the wavefunction

      - 'psinals' (integer, default='10'): number of ALS iterations for wavefunction rank reduction

      - 'hrank' (integer, default='0'): target rank for the Hamiltonian. Set hrank to '0' to skip
         reduction of the Hamiltonian (recommended, as reducing rank of H can cause errors in the energies)

      - 'hnals' (integer, default='200'): number of ALS iterations used in Hamiltonian rank reduction

      - 'alspenalty' (float, default='1.d-10'): regularization penalty when solving linear system
         during ALS

       -als_linsys_alg (string, default='LU'): algorithm used to solve linear systems during ALS
           'LU': use LU-decomposition
           'SVD' use Singular Value Decomposition

